1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,796
The customer's own screen.

2
00:00:01,796 --> 00:00:04,076
One business, Ridgeline Robotics.

3
00:00:04,076 --> 00:00:07,461
Availability here is not a column anybody writes.

4
00:00:07,461 --> 00:00:22,729
It's five terms, subtracted in front of you: sixty-
seven seven fifty-nine booked, less twenty-four six
ten waiting to settle, less twenty-nine seven-oh-one

5
00:00:22,729 --> 00:00:25,769
Ten thousand nine forty-seven, ninety-three.

6
00:00:25,769 --> 00:00:32,539
Read the line under it — that figure is the
database's own answer, not a total this page added
up.

7
00:00:32,539 --> 00:00:37,858
If the page and the ledger disagreed, the page would
be the one that's wrong.

8
00:00:37,858 --> 00:00:40,000
So the page doesn't get a vote.

9
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:43,246
A fuel pump authorises fifty dollars.

10
00:00:43,246 --> 00:00:46,228
It settles at seventy-three forty.

11
00:00:46,228 --> 00:00:58,772
The row says exactly that: held fifty when it was
authorised, took seventy-three forty when it settled
— twenty-three forty more than expected.

12
00:00:58,772 --> 00:01:05,526
The customer doesn't reconcile anything; the
difference is named on the line.

13
00:01:05,526 --> 00:01:16,228
Above it, the same clearing taken back and re-
presented — a reversal tagged as a correction, and
the refund leg beside it.

14
00:01:16,228 --> 00:01:20,000
Three rows, one story, nothing overwritten.

15
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:21,686
The twenty-fifth of July.

16
00:01:21,686 --> 00:01:24,250
A day that closed, then got corrected.

17
00:01:24,250 --> 00:01:33,558
Four legs: the funding credit, the original
clearing, the reversal that took it back, the re-
presentation at a hundred ninety-eight fifty.

18
00:01:33,558 --> 00:01:41,653
Now the three hashes — rebuilt just now, rebuilt
again a moment later, and the fingerprint stored the
day it was issued.

19
00:01:41,653 --> 00:01:42,327
Identical.

20
00:01:42,327 --> 00:01:50,826
The day is frozen at booking position nine eighty-
two, so rebuilding from the ledger there gives the
same document every time.

21
00:01:50,826 --> 00:01:55,413
And both earlier versions of this day, still issued,
still readable.

22
00:01:55,413 --> 00:02:00,000
A corrected statement is a new document, not an edit
of the old one.

23
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:01,721
Same window, same tab.

24
00:02:01,721 --> 00:02:03,599
This is the ops console.

25
00:02:03,599 --> 00:02:08,841
Priya Raman raised the top payment, so Approve and
Reject are live.

26
00:02:08,841 --> 00:02:16,743
Dana Okonkwo raised the one below, and Dana is
signed in — tagged "that is you" — so both are
greyed.

27
00:02:16,743 --> 00:02:17,447
Read why.

28
00:02:17,447 --> 00:02:28,792
The initiator is never the checker, and this is not
a rule the screen is applying:
assert_maker_checker() refuses the insert with

29
00:02:28,792 --> 00:02:32,000
Turn the UI off and the rule still holds.

30
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:34,338
One break, shown rather than swept.

31
00:02:34,338 --> 00:02:36,409
In the ledger, not in the file.

32
00:02:36,409 --> 00:02:37,211
Unexplained.

33
00:02:37,211 --> 00:02:40,017
Two hundred forty seventy-one outstanding.

34
00:02:40,017 --> 00:02:51,841
The age reads four hundred fifty-three days ahead,
because an unexplained break is aged from its value
date — how long the book has been wrong, and every

35
00:02:51,841 --> 00:02:55,983
A break already under correction is aged from when
we learned.

36
00:02:55,983 --> 00:02:59,324
Two clocks, and the screen says which one it used.

37
00:02:59,324 --> 00:03:04,000
"The engine reported one; this screen classifies
them and hides none."

38
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:04,854
Webhooks off.

39
00:03:04,854 --> 00:03:06,101
Settlement delayed.

40
00:03:06,101 --> 00:03:07,545
Deliveries duplicated.

41
00:03:07,545 --> 00:03:13,783
Settlement arriving before the authorisation it
belongs to — each armed against our own outbox.

42
00:03:13,783 --> 00:03:22,581
Below them, the invariants: the same views dbcheck
asserts, each of which must return zero rows,
measured live rather than remembered.

43
00:03:22,581 --> 00:03:24,222
Every entry sums to zero.

44
00:03:24,222 --> 00:03:26,455
A released hold withholds nothing.

45
00:03:26,455 --> 00:03:29,278
No day re-priced by a rate that came later.

46
00:03:29,278 --> 00:03:34,662
And one red, left on screen — three hundred eleven
rows under v_refused_auth_hold.

47
00:03:34,662 --> 00:03:43,000
It's on the register with a written argument, and
it's showing because a dashboard that only renders
its greens isn't evidence.
